WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.750
Getting to where we are familiar with
the language, with the intonation, with

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.420
the pronunciation, with the word choices.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.340
This is all part of learning language.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.190
It's all part of our brains
getting used to the language.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:16.530
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today, I want to talk a little

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.570
philosophically about language learning.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.260
I want to talk about how we
get to know a language, how

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.640
we become familiar with them.

00:00:24.855 --> 00:00:27.855
Uh, not necessarily how we learn
the language, because that's a

00:00:27.855 --> 00:00:29.325
big part of learning the language.

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:34.665
So remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, um, click

00:00:34.665 --> 00:00:36.285
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:36.285 --> 00:00:40.605
And of course, if you follow
me on, uh, any of the podcast,

00:00:40.605 --> 00:00:44.985
um, websites, then please leave
a re uh, you know, uh, review.

00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:49.035
So, you know, I like to talk
here about language learning.

00:00:49.065 --> 00:00:52.864
I know that many of my videos are
used by people who are learning

00:00:52.864 --> 00:00:55.995
Enlgish so I try to speak clearly.

00:00:56.745 --> 00:01:02.144
Uh, I think my Canadian accent is a
somewhat neutral, so it's probably

00:01:02.144 --> 00:01:03.435
easy for people to understand.

00:01:04.155 --> 00:01:07.785
And I talk about language learning
because I want to encourage

00:01:07.785 --> 00:01:09.675
people to learn languages.

00:01:09.705 --> 00:01:15.305
And I want to sort of, uh, make language
learning seem less of a difficult thing.

00:01:16.515 --> 00:01:19.935
Uh, I think if you follow these
videos, you'll know that I sort of,

00:01:20.715 --> 00:01:25.245
um, encourage people to treat language
learning as sort of a natural activity.

00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:28.275
Something that if we put in
enough time, we will improve.

00:01:28.725 --> 00:01:30.555
Uh, we don't have to get everything right.

00:01:30.555 --> 00:01:33.345
We don't have to get a big, you
know, a high score on the test.

00:01:34.635 --> 00:01:39.015
As I do try to avoid tests,
try to avoid drills exercises,

00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:41.085
try to just learn the language.

00:01:41.535 --> 00:01:46.665
And a big part of that is this sense of
becoming familiar with the language so

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:48.765
that the language becomes like a friend.

00:01:48.795 --> 00:01:52.875
So you get to know the language,
just like you get to know, you

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:55.995
get very familiar with people
in your family, familiar family.

00:01:55.995 --> 00:01:59.265
You get to know your friends, you
get to know their characteristics.

00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:01.535
Um, when you see them.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.720
You know how they behave, you
know, what their character is like.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.330
So you become very sort of
intimately connected with them.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.620
And I feel the same way with
the languages that I've learned.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.820
Uh, maybe it's because
I do a lot of listening.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:22.110
So, you know, I'm not deliberately trying
to learn anything I do when I'm, you know,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.580
after listening, I review it on my iPad
and look at words and stuff, but I'm not

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.170
deliberately trying to learn anything.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:33.450
I'm more trying to become friends with
the language I'm trying to start to

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.480
get in the mood to enjoy the language.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.540
And I think that helps in terms of
pronunciation, for example, in terms

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.630
of intonation, in terms of using the
right phrase, Uh, as we start to become

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.489
familiar with the language, even if
we don't fully understand it, a lot of

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:55.929
these things are kind of penetrating us.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:03.000
I noticed this in my Arabic and my,
and my Persian, uh, not paradoxically.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.130
And remember, again, that many of you are
studying languages at school, or you may

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.885
be studying them for professional reasons,
which was my case when I was learning

00:03:12.915 --> 00:03:15.375
Chinese 50 years ago, that was my job.

00:03:15.405 --> 00:03:19.365
I was doing it seven hours a day,
but now I'm doing it as a hobby.

00:03:20.205 --> 00:03:21.915
I'm doing it out of interest.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:26.265
I'm doing it because I'm interested,
not only in the language, but in

00:03:26.265 --> 00:03:30.135
the history and the culture behind
that language and possibly travel

00:03:30.135 --> 00:03:31.665
and food and meeting people.

00:03:31.665 --> 00:03:37.365
And that whole sense of being able to
participate in another cultural media.

00:03:38.775 --> 00:03:40.215
So that's what motivates me.

00:03:40.815 --> 00:03:46.245
And what I found recently is that my
strategy of, you know, and, and this

00:03:46.245 --> 00:03:49.245
is this whole issue of studying more
than one language, say two languages

00:03:49.245 --> 00:03:53.325
at the same time, you know, what's
the best way to combine languages.

00:03:53.325 --> 00:03:57.435
Should we be doing two languages
the same day or alternating days?

00:03:57.915 --> 00:04:02.445
And I'm beginning to find that if I
go one week on one and one week on the

00:04:02.445 --> 00:04:08.385
other, then when I come back to the first
one, I'm sort of reconnecting with an

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:11.325
old friend and that's a nice feeling.

00:04:11.415 --> 00:04:15.075
And I mean, even finding, and
as I said before, this is a

00:04:15.075 --> 00:04:16.785
business of benign neglect.

00:04:16.785 --> 00:04:20.925
If you leave one language, go to the other
one for awhile, come back to the first

00:04:20.925 --> 00:04:23.265
one, all of a sudden things are clearer.

00:04:23.985 --> 00:04:28.215
Um, maybe because you've pushed your
brain to work this other language.

00:04:28.915 --> 00:04:31.875
And maybe because if we stay with
the one language and we're trying

00:04:31.875 --> 00:04:34.675
to learn and we're disappointed that
we're not doing better in a certain

00:04:34.675 --> 00:04:37.695
way, frustration, creeps, creeps in.

00:04:38.085 --> 00:04:42.284
Whereas if we leave it for awhile, do
this other language and then we come

00:04:42.284 --> 00:04:47.025
back to the first one we rediscover an
old friend, everything is fresh again.

00:04:47.055 --> 00:04:50.924
And I do find that I
hear things more clearly.

00:04:51.405 --> 00:04:57.315
Uh, I I'm a little more aware of, of,
of the language and getting to where

00:04:57.315 --> 00:05:00.734
we are familiar with the language, with
the intonation, with the pronunciation.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.640
Word choices.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.530
This is all part of learning language.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.380
It's all part of our brains
getting used to that language.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:13.740
And if we can develop this sort of
natural, getting to know the language,

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:17.940
we're going to speak more naturally, as
opposed to trying to learn it through

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.540
rules and tests and things of that nature.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:23.790
And it's surprising.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.000
I was, uh, I spent a while
with, with, uh, Persian.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.200
Then I started listening to my,
um, Arabic, uh, podcast again.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:36.750
And I find that, you know, I recognize
my old friend, I recognize some of

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.469
the personality of the Arab speaker.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.590
He's sort of very intent on getting
his view across and he uses certain

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:48.090
expressions in a, uh, certainly in
the, uh, in the standard Arabic,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.729
which I'm listening to from, you
know, these political podcasts.

00:05:51.359 --> 00:05:56.669
Uh, the Egyptian Arabic has a different
personality and I have a very good tutor

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.890
who's creating content for me in Egypt.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.020
And so I can get familiar with and
get to know and get to feel the

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.910
personality of Egyptian Arabic.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:10.740
And then I'll leave that and
then I'll go to my Persian again.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:15.150
And on each of these occasions,
it's staying fresh for me.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:17.150
And then I'm rediscovering an old friend.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:24.090
Picking up where we left off and I find
that I'm progressing perhaps or enjoying

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:30.510
it more or less frustrated by my lack
of progress and more aware that I am

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.090
becoming more familiar with the language.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:34.770
It's becoming more familiar to me.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.930
It's becoming more of a friend to me.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.180
I'm recognizing characteristics.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.350
Therefore intonation,
pronunciation, turns of phrases.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.040
And in that way, the language
is gradually penetrating.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:53.280
And this normally, and I found this
when, after an absence, I started

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:57.690
speaking again with, with my tutor, all
of a sudden, all of this familiarity,

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:02.219
this comfort level with the language
as being, you know, converts itself

00:07:02.219 --> 00:07:06.599
into being able to speak more fleet
freely and speak more, I wouldn't say

00:07:06.599 --> 00:07:09.659
fluently yet in those languages, but
more comfortably in those languages.

00:07:09.659 --> 00:07:13.830
So, you know, learning languages
is not just learning the rules,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.390
although it can help to review the
basic patterns of the language.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.695
It's it's becoming, you know, emotionally
connected to the language, becoming

00:07:23.055 --> 00:07:25.965
more familiar with the language,
making the language your friend.

00:07:26.385 --> 00:07:31.815
So I find a strategy of alternating
languages works for me, but some

00:07:31.815 --> 00:07:34.725
people may prefer to stay with
one language, but ultimately we

00:07:34.725 --> 00:07:37.425
want to sort of get that language.

00:07:38.475 --> 00:07:43.005
And so I think learning language includes
this sense of making these languages

00:07:43.155 --> 00:07:45.495
your friend, making them familiar to you.

00:07:45.705 --> 00:07:48.645
So a little bit of a vague thought,
but it's something that's been running

00:07:48.645 --> 00:07:52.935
around and in my mind, and I'd be
interested in, in any reaction.

00:07:52.965 --> 00:07:56.295
Maybe what I had to say there
is, is just a lot of nonsense.

00:07:56.325 --> 00:07:56.925
I don't know.

00:07:57.255 --> 00:07:58.575
Anyway, thanks for listening.

